          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"flaubert-madame-bovary-8fe83e2776a5\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":1,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.91,\"analysis\":\"Rodolphe directly presents his love for Emma as true in response to her demand for reassurance. The exclamation mark and emphatic “Mais oui” add affective emphasis, but the utterance remains a clear avowal rather than a primarily reflexive exclamation. It does not itself undertake a commitment or obligation.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The emphatic form gives limited E support, while the wider relationship raises doubts about sincerity; neither factor displaces the utterance's dominant truth-conditional force.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Direct dialogue spoken by Rodolphe to Emma, embedded in narration; the imperfect “répondait-il” presents it as a characteristic or repeated response.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The reply functions as reassurance when Emma seeks confirmation of Rodolphe's love. “Mais oui” can sound mildly impatient or formulaic as well as emphatic, and the wider novel casts doubt on the depth and durability of Rodolphe's declarations. Possible insincerity, habituation, or manipulation concerns the reliability and context of the avowal, not its core T/P/E force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"ev1-local-avowal\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Mais oui, je t’aime!”\",\"supports\":\"The first-person present-tense declaration explicitly presents the speaker's love for the addressee as true, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev2-local-response\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“répondait-il”\",\"supports\":\"Marks the declaration as a spoken reply; the imperfect also suggests a recurring or characteristic reassurance rather than a singular new undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.93},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev3-local-emphasis\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The emphatic opening “Mais oui” and exclamation mark.\",\"supports\":\"Provides limited E support through emphatic, emotionally marked delivery, though clear propositional force remains dominant.\",\"confidence\":0.9},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev4-background-relationship\",\"source\":\"background_knowledge\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"In the surrounding Rodolphe–Emma affair, Emma repeatedly seeks assurances while Rodolphe's conduct and Flaubert's framing undermine confidence in the depth or durability of his professed passion.\",\"supports\":\"Explains the reply's reassuring and potentially formulaic or insincere context without converting that contextual doubt into O or eliminating T.\",\"confidence\":0.86}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":true,\"familiarity\":\"extensive\",\"confidence\":0.86,\"contribution\":\"Used to identify the speaker and addressee as Rodolphe and Emma and to interpret the declaration as recurrent reassurance within an affair whose sincerity is narratively questioned.\"},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the explicit avowal, with minor E support for emphatic delivery. No substantial undertaking force or residual core function requires P or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
